# {{PROJECT_NAME}} Deep Context routing

This project stores durable context in human-readable files. Chats and active context windows are disposable.

## Startup

1. Read `CONTEXT_MAP.md`.
2. Read `PROJECT.md` and `work/INDEX.md`; canonical item frontmatter owns work state.
3. When a repository is attached, resolve the source checkout from `.local/state.json` and read its `AGENTS.md` if present and follow its required source reads, live schedule, and ownership constraints.
4. If inside `tasks/<task>/`, read the task's `AGENTS.md`, `TASK_CONTEXT.md`, `CONTEXT_MANIFEST.md`, and `TASK_STATE.md`.
5. Read the generated indexes in `docs/` to select canonical files, including decisions and reports when deep context is needed.
6. Read only relevant canonical bodies. Deep context includes only decisions and reports associated with the selected feature; do not recursively load `docs/`.

{{TASK_ROUTING}}

## Preservation

Write durable architecture, contracts, requirements, and decisions under `docs/`, even if no task is active. Capture future ideas as atomic proposed records under `work/items/` with an elevator pitch. Read `work/INDEX.md` at planning or scope changes; explicitly resolve opposing intent before accepting a plan. Checkpoint active work before ending or handing off. Task folders are disposable only after deep-close promotes durable knowledge.

## Authority

Architecture, accepted requirements, and contracts outrank active task criteria; accepted work items outrank proposed work items; proposed work intent outranks speculation. Existing repository-specific routing and live schedules retain their declared authority.
